  "live_records": {
    "q-106414": {
      "dom_id": "step-q106414",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14222477/assets/8934e5b6135b97b845dad9a6b87056d2c228fd2424d3a72141df694d46ccf7c0.png)\n\nWhich of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ generate the parabola shown above?",
      "worked_solution": "The diagram shows that the parabola is vertical, has its vertex at $(0,0),$ and passes through the point $(-6,12).$\n\nThe parametric equations of a vertical parabola  that has its vertex at $(0,0)$ are\n\n$$\nx=2pt,\\,y=p{t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(-6,12),$ we can substitute $x=-6$ and $y=12$ into the standard parametric equations to obtain a system of equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-6=2pt \\\\ 12=p{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-6 & =2pt \\\\ -3 & =pt \\\\ t & =-\\frac{3}{p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12 & =p{(-\\frac{3}{p})}^{2} \\\\ 12 & =p\\cdot \\frac{9}{{p}^{2}} \\\\ 12 & =\\frac{9}{p} \\\\ p & =\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx=2(\\frac{3}{4})t,\\,y=(\\frac{3}{4}){t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{3}{2}t,\\,y=\\frac{3}{4}{t}^{2}.\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
