    "q-84731": {
      "dom_id": "step-q84731",
      "name": "Question 13",
      "problem": "Find the Cartesian equation of the parabola defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{4}{t}^{2},\\,y=\\frac{1}{2}t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "First, let's make $t$ the subject of the parametric equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & =4x,\\,t=2y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquaring both sides of the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & =4{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now eliminate the parameter $t$ by substituting the above into the equation ${t}^{2}=4x$ and rearranging to find the Cartesian equation of the parabola, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{y}^{2} & =4x \\\\ {y}^{2} & =x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian equation of the parabola is ${y}^{2}=x.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
            {
              "option": "a",
              "dom_id": "questionWidget-choiceLetterCircle-84731-a",
